.\" mutos_cpp.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross C preprocessor
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_cpp.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_cpp.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_CPP 1 "September 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_cpp \- MUTOS 1700 cross C preprocessor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_cpp
.RI [ \-D name [= value ]]\ ...
.RI [ \-U name ]\ ...
.RI [ \-I dir ]\ ...
.RB [ \-P ]
.RB [ \-C ]
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RI [ infile
.RI [ outfile ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_cpp
performs macro expansion, file inclusion, and conditional compilation
on C (and MUTOS assembler) source, exactly as the C compiler's first
pass does on the original MUTOS 1700 hardware toolchain. It
re-implements the observable behavior of that toolchain's own
preprocessor \(en itself a fast rewrite by John F. Reiser of the
original Version 7 Unix
.BR cpp (1)
\(en from scratch in modern C, validated byte-for-byte against real
reference output; see
.I src/mutos_cpp/README.md
in the toolchain source tree for the full reverse-engineered
behavioral specification this implementation follows, including its
deliberate K&R-era departures from ANSI/ISO
.BR cpp (1)
behavior.
.PP
Source is read from
.IR infile ,
or from the standard input if
.I infile
is omitted. Output is written to
.IR outfile ,
or to the file given with
.BR \-o ,
or to the standard output if neither is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-D name
Define
.I name
as if by
.RI "\(dq#define\ " name "\ 1\(dq" .
.TP
.BI \-D name = value
Define
.I name
as if by
.RI "\(dq#define\ " name "\ " value \(dq.
.TP
.BI \-U name
Remove any built-in or previously
.B \-D ' d
definition of
.IR name .
.TP
.BI \-I dir
Add
.I dir
to the list of directories searched for
.RI \(dq< name >\(dq-style
.B #include
files, and for
.RI \(dq\(dq name \(dq\(dq-style
includes not found relative to the including file's own directory.
Directories are searched in the order given.
.TP
.B \-P
Accepted for command-line compatibility with the reference
.BR cpp (1).
.B mutos_cpp
never emits
.RI \(dq# " N " \(dq file \(dq\(dq
line-marker output regardless of this flag \(en every real MUTOS 1700
golden reference this tool is validated against was itself generated
with
.B \-P
and contains no such markers, so this is simply this tool's only
supported mode.
.TP
.B \-C
Keep comments in macro-expanded output instead of deleting them.
Accepted for compatibility; the real MUTOS 1700 kernel source this
toolchain targets does not rely on this flag.
.TP
.B \-R
Accepted for compatibility with the reference
.BR cpp (1) 's
recursive-macro-expansion flag. Self-referential macros are always
protected against infinite recursion regardless of this flag (see
.B DIAGNOSTICS ),
so it has no additional effect here.
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write output to
.I outfile
instead of standard output.
.SH "PREPROCESSING LANGUAGE NOTES"
.B mutos_cpp
implements the K&R-era preprocessing language of the original MUTOS
1700 toolchain, not the later ANSI/ISO C preprocessor. In particular:
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B #elif
\(en only
.BR #if / #ifdef / #ifndef ,
a single optional
.BR #else ,
and
.BR #endif .
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.B #
(stringize) or
.B ##
(token-paste) operators.
.IP \(bu 2
A bare identifier inside a
.B #if
expression tests whether it is currently
.BR #define d
(evaluating to 1 or 0 accordingly) rather than being replaced by its
macro value first;
.RI \(dq defined( name )\(dq
and
.RI \(dq defined\ name \(dq
evaluate to that identical 0/1 result.
.IP \(bu 2
.B #
is only recognized as introducing a directive when it is the very
first character of a physical line; a
.B #
preceded by any whitespace is treated as ordinary text.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors and warnings are printed to standard error as
.RI \(dq file : line : message \(dq.
Warnings (for example, a macro redefinition, or an actual/formal
parameter-count mismatch in a macro call) are non-fatal.  Errors
(for example, an unterminated
.BR #if / #ifdef / #ifndef ,
an unterminated comment or string/character literal, or a missing
.BR #include
file) cause
.B mutos_cpp
to exit with a non-zero status, though processing continues to the
end of input so that further problems in the same source can be
reported in one pass.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_as (1),
.BR mutos_ld (1)
